WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:02.300
-I'm happy to see you in person.

00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:04.968
I think the last time we talked,
you were 10 days away

00:00:04.968 --> 00:00:06.601
from having
your third grandchild.

00:00:06.601 --> 00:00:07.801
-That's right.
That's right.

00:00:07.801 --> 00:00:09.734
That's how we time
how long it's been.

00:00:09.734 --> 00:00:14.868
It was in the middle of 2020.

00:00:14.868 --> 00:00:17.434
My beautiful
little granddaughter, Una,

00:00:17.434 --> 00:00:24.033
my third grandchild -- she was
born just before July 4, 2020.

00:00:24.033 --> 00:00:26.701
And I was on the show with you.
-It's been two years already?

00:00:26.701 --> 00:00:28.400
-Yeah.
-It goes by kind of fast,

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:29.734
doesn't it?
-Wonderful.

00:00:29.734 --> 00:00:32.934
And that dark year,
she was the little bright star.

00:00:32.934 --> 00:00:34.634
-Yeah.
[ Audience "Awws" ]

00:00:34.634 --> 00:00:36.133
You must be -- come on.

00:00:36.133 --> 00:00:40.367
You must be the most fun grandpa
in the world, or Papa?

00:00:40.367 --> 00:00:44.834
What do they call you?
-She calls me Papa.

00:00:44.834 --> 00:00:48.634
She's just as delightful for me
as I am for her, far more.

00:00:48.634 --> 00:00:49.801
[ Laughter ]
-Oh, I know, yeah.

00:00:49.801 --> 00:00:53.133
But, yeah, you're artistic.
You paint.

00:00:53.133 --> 00:00:55.367
-Well, I also
entertain children.

00:00:55.367 --> 00:00:57.434
You know, I've been on your show
doing kid songs.

00:00:57.434 --> 00:01:00.200
-That's right.
Do you have any new songs

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:01.334
that you sing to her
that she likes?

00:01:01.334 --> 00:01:03.767
-Well, she has a favorite.

00:01:03.767 --> 00:01:06.267
She's already
picking her favorites.

00:01:06.267 --> 00:01:08.801
She's always asked
for the "Bee Song,"

00:01:08.801 --> 00:01:11.667
which is a song
my father sang to me

00:01:11.667 --> 00:01:14.434
and his mother sang to him.

00:01:14.434 --> 00:01:16.467
So I guess it's been around
a long time.

00:01:16.467 --> 00:01:17.567
-I don't know the "Bee Song."

00:01:17.567 --> 00:01:19.467
-I will --
I'll sing it for you, Jimmy.

00:01:19.467 --> 00:01:24.334
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:24.334 --> 00:01:27.667
But to really appreciate
the impact of this song,

00:01:27.667 --> 00:01:30.267
you have to pretend
that you're all 2 years old.

00:01:30.267 --> 00:01:31.434
[ Laughter ]

00:01:31.434 --> 00:01:35.400
♪ oh, I wish I were
a rhinocenario ♪

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:38.501
♪ Hippopatamie,
aha-ha-ha-ha ♪

00:01:38.501 --> 00:01:39.868
[ Laughter ]

00:01:39.868 --> 00:01:43.634
♪ But since I'm not
and never can hope to be ♪

00:01:43.634 --> 00:01:48.767
♪ Hippopotamius,
rhinocenarie ♪

00:01:48.767 --> 00:01:51.167
♪ I'm a June bug ♪

00:01:51.167 --> 00:01:53.567
♪ I'm a bumblebee ♪

00:01:53.567 --> 00:01:56.434
♪ I buzz and hit my head
against the tree ♪

00:01:56.434 --> 00:01:57.501
♪ Aha-ha-ha-ha ♪

00:01:57.501 --> 00:01:59.534
[ Laughter ]
There we go.

00:01:59.534 --> 00:02:00.601
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:00.601 --> 00:02:01.801
-Come on, you know how to do it.

00:02:01.801 --> 00:02:03.267
-She absolutely loves this song.

00:02:03.267 --> 00:02:06.067
-I love that song, too.
I love that.

00:02:06.067 --> 00:02:09.501
-You can see why I get
this requested constantly.

00:02:09.501 --> 00:02:10.934
-Yeah.
You are a great --

00:02:10.934 --> 00:02:12.133
I mean,
you do entertain children.

00:02:12.133 --> 00:02:14.801
Do you still do that
or go out there and --

00:02:14.801 --> 00:02:16.367
-Yeah, well,
I'm sort of reviving it

00:02:16.367 --> 00:02:20.467
now again
with a little baby nearby.

00:02:20.467 --> 00:02:25.434
I do kid songs, and I'm famous
for my animal sounds.

00:02:25.434 --> 00:02:27.701
-Oh.
What animal sounds?

00:02:27.701 --> 00:02:29.234
-The song, "I Had a Rooster."

00:02:29.234 --> 00:02:30.767
Don't worry. I'm not gonna
sing another song.

00:02:30.767 --> 00:02:31.901
[ Laughter ]

00:02:31.901 --> 00:02:33.901
But you run through
all the barnyard animals

00:02:33.901 --> 00:02:36.167
and make their noises.
And among little,

00:02:36.167 --> 00:02:38.000
little children,
I'm very famous for that.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:42.300
The cow goes, "Mooooooo."

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:44.033
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:02:44.033 --> 00:02:47.334
-That's an advanced cow.
-Yeah.

00:02:47.334 --> 00:02:49.267
And the horse goes --

00:02:49.267 --> 00:02:50.901
[ Whinnies ]

00:02:50.901 --> 00:02:53.133
[ Laughter ]

00:02:53.133 --> 00:02:54.934
-Come on, that's what you want.

00:02:54.934 --> 00:02:56.167
That's what you want
right there.

00:02:56.167 --> 00:02:57.434
-I've been trying to figure out

00:02:57.434 --> 00:03:00.300
where to work that in
to serious drama.

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:01.968
[ Laughter ]
-Oh.

00:03:01.968 --> 00:03:04.801
Talk to me. Next script,
I'll work it in somehow.

00:03:04.801 --> 00:03:08.334
I want to go through a few of
your roles just off the top --

00:03:08.334 --> 00:03:10.834
I made a little list of things.
You played a prime minister --

00:03:10.834 --> 00:03:12.033
you were Churchill.

00:03:12.033 --> 00:03:14.300
You played a president --
"Hillary and Clinton."

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:15.634
A reverend in "Footloose."

00:03:15.634 --> 00:03:18.100
A serial killer, "Dexter" --
by the way, unbelievable.

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:20.734
A scientist,
"Manhattan Project."

00:03:20.734 --> 00:03:22.234
A painter, "Love is Strange."

00:03:22.234 --> 00:03:25.334
And an alien,
"Third Rock from the Sun."

00:03:25.334 --> 00:03:29.200
I was asking,
do people ever go up to you and,

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:30.934
like, not talk to you
like you're John Lithgow

00:03:30.934 --> 00:03:32.834
and go like,
"Oh, you're Winston Churchill,"

00:03:32.834 --> 00:03:35.067
or, "You're..."
-Not really.

00:03:35.067 --> 00:03:37.801
Actually, when I play
a really bad character,

00:03:37.801 --> 00:03:41.868
like the Trinity Killer,
or, you know, my villains,

00:03:41.868 --> 00:03:45.167
they tend to shy away from me.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:45.167 --> 00:03:49.234
In fact, I was in --
I played a horrifying character,

00:03:49.234 --> 00:03:53.634
a psychopathic character
in Brian de Palma's "Blow Out"

00:03:53.634 --> 00:03:55.434
in the '80s.
-Oh, yeah.

00:03:55.434 --> 00:03:58.734
-And the weekend it opened
in New York,

00:03:58.734 --> 00:04:01.767
I was living
on the Upper West Side.

00:04:01.767 --> 00:04:04.834
The garbage bin
in my apartment building

00:04:04.834 --> 00:04:07.868
was just opposite
the service elevator.

00:04:07.868 --> 00:04:10.000
And everybody always used
the service elevator,

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:13.200
since it was so much faster than
the usual -- the regular one.

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:18.000
-Sure.
-So, just as I was emptying

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:23.634
the garbage, the service
elevator door opened behind me.

00:04:23.634 --> 00:04:26.100
I turned around.
There was a woman who lived on

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:27.534
the same floor,
although I'd never

00:04:27.534 --> 00:04:29.367
seen her before, nor she me.

00:04:29.367 --> 00:04:35.400
She saw me and turned white, and
practically had a heart attack.

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:37.167
[ Laughter ]
"Aaah!"

00:04:37.167 --> 00:04:40.334
She had just been
to see "Blow Out,"

00:04:40.334 --> 00:04:43.534
and there I was at about 9:30 --
[ Laughter ]

00:04:43.534 --> 00:04:45.601
-By a dumpster.
By a dumpster.

00:04:45.601 --> 00:04:49.033
-And I went to her like,
"Oh!"

00:04:49.033 --> 00:04:52.000
I only made it worse!
-You're making it worse!

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:53.934
"Calm down, calm down."

00:04:53.934 --> 00:04:56.667
-It's an occupational hazard.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:56.667 --> 00:04:58.634
This happens from time to time.

00:04:58.634 --> 00:05:00.067
-Let's talk about "The Old Man"

00:05:00.067 --> 00:05:02.634
because I'm very excited
about this.

00:05:02.634 --> 00:05:05.434
You know, we love Jeff Bridges
here on our show, as well.

00:05:05.434 --> 00:05:08.767
And I know this kind of got --
it got pushed.

00:05:08.767 --> 00:05:09.901
There was all sorts of problems,

00:05:09.901 --> 00:05:11.434
and different things
that came down.

00:05:11.434 --> 00:05:13.968
But, finally, it's finished.
-It's finished.

00:05:13.968 --> 00:05:16.467
It opens -- actually, it's
on tonight for the first time.

00:05:16.467 --> 00:05:18.267
-Yes.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:18.267 --> 00:05:21.467
-And it's really, really good.

00:05:21.467 --> 00:05:25.400
This show started
2 1/2 years ago.

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:27.300
-Yeah.
-And we got about

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:29.033
2 1/2 months of it done.

00:05:29.033 --> 00:05:31.968
We finished about four episodes.

00:05:31.968 --> 00:05:33.400
Then came COVID.

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:35.767
And about eight months passed.

00:05:35.767 --> 00:05:38.300
We were just about
ready to start again.

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:41.934
Then came
poor Jeff's cancer diagnosis.

00:05:41.934 --> 00:05:42.934
-Yes.

00:05:42.934 --> 00:05:46.300
-And that took
another 10 months.

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:48.834
He went through hell with it.

00:05:48.834 --> 00:05:51.133
He recovered.
He has survived.

00:05:51.133 --> 00:05:52.400
He's better.
-Yes!

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:54.300
-He's better than ever.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:56.634
-Exactly right.
-It's wonderful.

00:05:56.634 --> 00:05:58.200
-Have you guys
worked together before?

00:05:58.200 --> 00:05:59.667
-We had never
worked together before.

00:05:59.667 --> 00:06:02.934
We'd barely even met before.
And for those --

00:06:02.934 --> 00:06:08.000
that long 18-month stretch,
I had never acted with him yet

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:10.000
because it's,
in the nature of the series,

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:13.033
that our two characters
are sort of kept apart

00:06:13.033 --> 00:06:16.367
but drawn together over
the course of seven episodes.

00:06:16.367 --> 00:06:19.501
And we hadn't gotten to them
getting together yet.

00:06:19.501 --> 00:06:22.701
So, finally, February and March
of this year,

00:06:22.701 --> 00:06:28.400
I got to act with Jeff Bridges
and found him to be the --

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:30.267
as everybody would expect,

00:06:30.267 --> 00:06:34.734
the most wonderful man
and a terrific acting partner.

00:06:34.734 --> 00:06:37.501
This show starts great tonight.

00:06:37.501 --> 00:06:39.868
But just wait
for the seventh episode.

00:06:39.868 --> 00:06:41.934
It just gets better and better.

00:06:41.934 --> 00:06:45.868
It's as extraordinary thing.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:45.868 --> 00:06:48.501
-Is there any way to set it up,
this show?

00:06:48.501 --> 00:06:52.534
-Well, we -- we play two guys,
who, 30 years ago,

00:06:52.534 --> 00:06:55.534
in Afghanistan,
during the Soviet war,

00:06:55.534 --> 00:06:58.200
not the American
involvement there,

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:01.334
they were operatives in the CIA.

00:07:01.334 --> 00:07:05.133
I was sort of his handler,
and he was a kind of renegade

00:07:05.133 --> 00:07:07.100
who did some of the --

00:07:07.100 --> 00:07:09.968
some very bad things
for very good reasons,

00:07:09.968 --> 00:07:11.667
and it all blew up.

00:07:11.667 --> 00:07:14.167
And we were forced
to stay apart

00:07:14.167 --> 00:07:18.300
and sort of not even acknowledge
each other for 30 years.

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:20.934
And that event
comes back to haunt us

00:07:20.934 --> 00:07:22.934
and, as I say, pulls us back.

00:07:22.934 --> 00:07:24.501
We have to engage.

00:07:24.501 --> 00:07:27.100
And we play this long
cat-and-mouse game

00:07:27.100 --> 00:07:29.234
of international intrigue.

00:07:29.234 --> 00:07:33.334
But it's so -- it's so fine.

00:07:33.334 --> 00:07:36.000
I saw it up
on a big screen at our --

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:37.968
the other night,
and it's as good as any movie.

00:07:37.968 --> 00:07:39.701
It's wonderful.
-Oh, I love that.

00:07:39.701 --> 00:07:40.801
I want to show everyone a clip.

00:07:40.801 --> 00:07:42.667
Here's John Lithgow
in "The Old Man."

00:07:42.667 --> 00:07:44.434
Take a look at this.

00:07:44.434 --> 00:07:46.801
-He's on the move.

00:07:46.801 --> 00:07:49.100
-Yeah, I can see that.

00:07:49.100 --> 00:07:52.267
-Just minutes before
we arrived on target, too.

00:07:52.267 --> 00:07:54.567
-That's unfortunate.

00:07:56.501 --> 00:07:59.434
-You were just on the phone
out there, yeah?

00:07:59.434 --> 00:08:01.767
-Yes, I was.

00:08:01.767 --> 00:08:04.167
-May I ask who it was
you were talking to?

00:08:04.167 --> 00:08:06.167
-No, you may not.

00:08:09.133 --> 00:08:13.567
Son, do you know what
my position is at the FBI?

00:08:13.567 --> 00:08:15.167
-Yes, sir.

00:08:15.167 --> 00:08:17.033
-Do you think I was appointed
to that position

00:08:17.033 --> 00:08:20.467
because I'm somebody
that's safe to [bleep] with?

00:08:20.467 --> 00:08:21.934
-No, sir.

00:08:21.934 --> 00:08:24.534
-Then quit staring at me
before I start getting the idea

00:08:24.534 --> 00:08:26.067
I'm being [bleep] with.

00:08:26.067 --> 00:08:28.000
-Yeah, that's exactly right!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:30.734
What's up?!
Yes!

00:08:30.734 --> 00:08:33.467
John Lithgow!
Check out new episodes

00:08:33.467 --> 00:08:36.434
of "The Old Man"
every Thursday on FX

00:08:36.434 --> 00:08:38.234
and on Hulu the next day.

00:08:38.234 --> 00:08:40.167
We'll be right back
with Noah Schnapp.

00:08:40.167 --> 00:08:41.467
Stick around, everybody.

